{
  "case_id": "2022-21-1576",
  "document_hash": "542c70cadd503658bf37a6a7e8cbb130a67641246b8899b293815fde5d352660",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1576",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "542c70cadd503658bf37a6a7e8cbb130a67641246b8899b293815fde5d352660",
      "evidence_id": "ev-542c70cadd50-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 7,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE THOMAS: When we have -- normally have constitutional errors at trial, isn't our rule a -- a --a -- a mistrial or a retrial?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Samir Deger\u2011Sen",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1576",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "542c70cadd503658bf37a6a7e8cbb130a67641246b8899b293815fde5d352660",
      "evidence_id": "ev-542c70cadd50-0000-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 15,
        "start": 14
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And that's essentially what we're coming to court and asking for, is we want that judgment of acquittal. We want that piece of paper that says you can't reprosecute us. And the -- and what the Eleventh Circuit gave us is the opposite. It gave us a piece of paper that says the government can reprosecute you. You don't have the ordinary effects that you'd get with a judgment of acquittal."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1576",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "542c70cadd503658bf37a6a7e8cbb130a67641246b8899b293815fde5d352660",
      "evidence_id": "ev-542c70cadd50-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 15,
        "start": 15
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: Do you have any decision from the founding era that actually precluded retrial based on a prior verdict of improper venue?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Jackson",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1576",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "542c70cadd503658bf37a6a7e8cbb130a67641246b8899b293815fde5d352660",
      "evidence_id": "ev-542c70cadd50-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE JACKSON: Why can't the remedy\njust be to vacate the conviction? I mean, what\nyou're asking for is a vacatur plus a statement\nby the Court that you can't be retried, and I\nguess Justice Kagan's point is, why don't you\nget that one when the --when the government"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. DEGER-SEN",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1576",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "542c70cadd503658bf37a6a7e8cbb130a67641246b8899b293815fde5d352660",
      "evidence_id": "ev-542c70cadd50-0001-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. DEGER-SEN: I -- I just think\nthey're -- they're inseparably interwoven here\nbecause what it means for the venue right to be\nviolated is for the government to fail its\nburden of proof before a jury. That is what it\nhas always meant. There's no such thing as a\nvenue violation separate from the government's\nfailure of proof before a jury."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. DEGER-SEN",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1576",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "542c70cadd503658bf37a6a7e8cbb130a67641246b8899b293815fde5d352660",
      "evidence_id": "ev-542c70cadd50-0001-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. DEGER-SEN: I mean, the criminal\nappellate right has only existed, you know,\nsince 1891, so the early case, the common law\nand the founding, there was no criminal right of\nappeal. So this is sort of all a little bit sui\ngeneris."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1576",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "542c70cadd503658bf37a6a7e8cbb130a67641246b8899b293815fde5d352660",
      "evidence_id": "ev-542c70cadd50-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 28,
        "start": 28
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: So, if there's a\nmotion to dismiss for lack of venue and the\njudge grants it and there's an appeal by the\ngovernment and it's not a judgment of acquittal,\nthe government can then retry -- can try the\ncase, correct?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Gorsuch",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1576",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "542c70cadd503658bf37a6a7e8cbb130a67641246b8899b293815fde5d352660",
      "evidence_id": "ev-542c70cadd50-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GORSUCH: What do you\nunderstand the proper burden of proof of the\ngovernment to be in -- in venue cases?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Jackson",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1576",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "542c70cadd503658bf37a6a7e8cbb130a67641246b8899b293815fde5d352660",
      "evidence_id": "ev-542c70cadd50-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE JACKSON: No, but I guess --I guess, doesn't that -- why doesn't that undermine your argument? I mean, my -- if it is an acquittal, then it seems to me that the -- that there shouldn't be an appellate right for the government to go and ask for a review of the judge's determination that there's no venue on"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. DeGer-Sen",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1576",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "542c70cadd503658bf37a6a7e8cbb130a67641246b8899b293815fde5d352660",
      "evidence_id": "ev-542c70cadd50-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "a jury acquittal, a judge acquittal under Rule 29, and an -- an appellate reversal of a Rule 29 motion for insufficiency, those are all insufficiency determinations, and it's a purely arbitrary distinction to say that, you know, because the jury made a mistake, the government now suddenly gets to reprosecute."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Jackson",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1576",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "542c70cadd503658bf37a6a7e8cbb130a67641246b8899b293815fde5d352660",
      "evidence_id": "ev-542c70cadd50-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 47,
        "start": 47
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Except insofar as our law might allow the government to raise venue questions on appeal if ruled on by a judge."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Sopan Joshi",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1576",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "542c70cadd503658bf37a6a7e8cbb130a67641246b8899b293815fde5d352660",
      "evidence_id": "ev-542c70cadd50-0002-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 48,
        "start": 48
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The remedy for a defendant convicted in a wrong venue is, one, reversal of his conviction; two, dismissal of the count in the indictment. Petitioner got both of those remedies here. His contention here is that the Constitution requires an additional third remedy, which is immunity from reprosecution."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1576",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "542c70cadd503658bf37a6a7e8cbb130a67641246b8899b293815fde5d352660",
      "evidence_id": "ev-542c70cadd50-0002-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 51,
        "start": 51
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "times does the government, for example, get to either mistake or deliberately sue in the wrong venue?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1576",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "542c70cadd503658bf37a6a7e8cbb130a67641246b8899b293815fde5d352660",
      "evidence_id": "ev-542c70cadd50-0003-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 66,
        "start": 66
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: Does that apply --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1576",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "542c70cadd503658bf37a6a7e8cbb130a67641246b8899b293815fde5d352660",
      "evidence_id": "ev-542c70cadd50-0003-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 66,
        "start": 66
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Petitioner mentioned them when -- or, I'm sorry, my friend mentioned them when he was up here that, you know, special verdicts were never used."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1576",
      "confidence": 0.8,
      "document_hash": "542c70cadd503658bf37a6a7e8cbb130a67641246b8899b293815fde5d352660",
      "evidence_id": "ev-542c70cadd50-0003-0004",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 80,
        "start": 80
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "venue is inappropriate, in those circumstances, a retrial is permissible just --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Joshi",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1576",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "542c70cadd503658bf37a6a7e8cbb130a67641246b8899b293815fde5d352660",
      "evidence_id": "ev-542c70cadd50-0004-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 88,
        "start": 88
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the framers codified the venue and vicinage rights in the Constitution, they tweaked them a little bit, you know, they changed county to state and vicinage to district, but they didn't touch the remedial principles that accompany that right, that old soil remains intact."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Joshi",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1576",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "542c70cadd503658bf37a6a7e8cbb130a67641246b8899b293815fde5d352660",
      "evidence_id": "ev-542c70cadd50-0004-0002",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 89,
        "start": 89
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "No, we -- we have not."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Deger\u2011Sen",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1576",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "542c70cadd503658bf37a6a7e8cbb130a67641246b8899b293815fde5d352660",
      "evidence_id": "ev-542c70cadd50-0004-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 90,
        "start": 90
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The government agrees it's not jurisdictional. The framers clearly didn't incorporate the jurisdictional principles of English common law, certainly not jurisdictional principles of later English common law into the Constitution."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Deger\u2011Sen",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1576",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "542c70cadd503658bf37a6a7e8cbb130a67641246b8899b293815fde5d352660",
      "evidence_id": "ev-542c70cadd50-0004-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 92,
        "start": 92
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "If that's true, how could it possibly be the case the framers would have contemplated a remedy that did allow just at the government's discretion being shipped to London and then to Manchester or maybe even being retried in London multiple times."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1576",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "542c70cadd503658bf37a6a7e8cbb130a67641246b8899b293815fde5d352660",
      "evidence_id": "ev-542c70cadd50-0004-0006",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 94,
        "start": 94
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Thank you, counsel. The case is submitted."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2022-21-1576",
      "confidence": 0.0,
